  "raw_text": "Step 1: Given the equation \\(x^2 - x - 1 = 0\\), we can express \\(x^2\\) in terms of \\(x\\):\n\\[x^2 = x + 1\\]\n\nStep 2: We need to find the value of \\(x^3 - 2x + 1\\). First, express \\(x^3\\) using the relation from Step 1:\n\\[x^3 = x \\cdot x^2 = x(x + 1) = x^2 + x\\]\nSubstitute \\(x^2 = x + 1\\) into the expression for \\(x^3\\):\n\\[x^3 = (x + 1) + x = 2x + 1\\]\n\nStep 3: Substitute \\(x^3 = 2x + 1\\) and the original expression into \\(x^3 - 2x + 1\\):\n\\[x^3 - 2x + 1 = (2x + 1) - 2x + 1\\]\nSimplify the expression:\n\\[x^3 - 2x + 1 = 2x + 1 - 2x + 1 = 2\\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{2}<|im_end|>",
