    "answer": "2",
    "level": 4,
    "problem": "If $x^2 - x - 1 = 0$, what is the value of $x^3 - 2x + 1$?",
    "solution": "Rearranging $x^2 - x - 1= 0$ gives $x^2 = x + 1$.  So, repeatedly substituting $x+1$ for $x^2$ gives us \\begin{align*}\nx^3 - 2x + 1 &= x(x^2)-2x + 1\\\\\n&=x(x+1) - 2x + 1\\\\\n&= x^2 + x -2x + 1\\\\\n&= x^2 - x + 1\\\\\n&= (x+1) - x + 1\\\\\n&=\\boxed{2}\n\\end{align*}",
